  "BEST_WHY": "The north wall run of the lit room (x\u2248185\u2013730, y\u2248425\u2013450) holds a continuous dark base line across its full width, and it holds it *under* the light spill from the corridor mouth \u2014 measured, that seam sits at luminance 62 against a floor at 165, a 103-point separation, so the room reads as bounded rather than as a bright area that fades out. The driven pins and timber ties along the top of that run are spaced irregularly \u2014 a tie at x\u2248382, a pin at x\u2248500, a longer plank butting the corridor jamb at x\u2248545 \u2014 so the repair reads as done by hand over time, not as a stamped repeat. On the floor immediately south of the figure the slabs carry genuinely different base values (the pale slab at x\u2248430\u2013475, y\u2248490 sits ~40 points above its neighbour at x\u2248480\u2013530), so the stones read as individually cut and separately worn rather than as one texture with joint lines drawn on top. The floor's local variance is *higher* than image 1's (49.5 vs 42.6) while being darker overall, which means the lighting is being carried by the artwork instead of erasing it.",
  "WORST_WHY": "The water body (x\u2248248\u2013344, y\u22480\u2013190) is a perfect rectangle. Four right-angle corners, a dead-straight bottom edge at y\u2248190, and no bank, no wet margin, no value change where teal meets dirt \u2014 the water and the ground are butted like two swatches. The dirt/stone boundary on the left (x\u224856) and the bottom (y\u2248212) are the same: straight cuts on the grid with no transition tile in either direction. The two barrels at x\u2248118 and x\u2248160, y\u2248196 are the identical sprite pasted twice \u2014 same rim highlight, same stave shadow, same three flecks on the lid, no rotation or offset. The grey floor along the bottom is one tile repeated across the full width with the specular dot in the same place in every instance. And there is no light source anywhere: the frame is uniformly ambient, so nothing in it has a near side or a far side. Nothing here is *broken* \u2014 it is simply assembled rather than made, and it is the one frame that would be read as the free one on sight.",
